ly one `<h1>` whose normalized text is\\n   `Hello world`** — `tests/integration/frontend-home.spec.ts` does\\n   `page.goto('/')` then `expect(page.locator('h1')).toHaveText('Hello world')`.\\n   `locator('h1')` is strict: a second `<h1>` anywhere in the page (e.g. one\\n   added in the root layout) resolves to multiple elements and fails. The text\\n   match is exact after whitespace normalization — no extra words, no suffix,\\n   no nested markup adding text.\\n5. **Whole thing must come up inside 300 s** (`webServer.timeout: 300_000`),\\n   which covers image pull + dependency install + `next build`. Keep the image\\n   and the dependency set lean; avoid anything that makes a network call at\\n   build time beyond the package registry.\\n6. **`services/frontend/tsconfig.json` must exist and typecheck clean**, because\\n   the architecture's code quality gate points at it.\\n\\n## 3. Files to create (all under `services/frontend/`)\\n\\n### 3.1 `services/frontend/package.json` — *serves: `<startup>` (image build)*\\n\\nPrivate, non-published package for the Next.js App Router service.\\n\\n- `dependencies`: `next`, `react`, `react-dom` (Next 15.x line with React 19;\\n  pin to caret ranges that are mutually peer-compatible).\\n- `devDependencies`: `typescript`, `@types/node`, `@types/react`,\\n  `@types/react-dom`.\\n- `scripts`:\\n  - `build`: `next build`\\n  - `start`: `next start -H 0.0.0.0 -p 3000` — bind host explicitly rather than\\n    relying on defaults, to guarantee contract item 2.\\n  - `dev`: `next dev -H 0.0.0.0 -p 3000` (convenience only; the container runs\\n    `start`).\\n  - `typecheck`: `tsc --noEmit` — gives the architecture's typescript quality\\n    gate a stable entry point.\\n\\nAlso generate and commit **`services/frontend/package-lock.json`** by running\\n`npm install` once in `services/frontend`. Two reasons: the Dockerfile can then\\nuse `npm ci` for a reproducible, faster install (helps the 300 s budget), and\\nthe host-side typescr